---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import Figure from "../components/Figure.astro";
import LoopDiagram from "../components/LoopDiagram.astro";
import { courseMeta, prerequisites, sequel } from "../course-config";
import { midSemesterBreak, teachingEnds } from "../lib/calendar";
import { constraints, sites, household } from "../lib/model";
import { formatCourseDate } from "../lib/dates";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";

const worst = sites.reduce((a, b) =>
  a.reference.excellent.cost_headroom < b.reference.excellent.cost_headroom ? a : b,
);
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <p class="claim">
    Growing a near-complete diet takes something like 372 m² of good ground per
    adult. The largest site in this course has 500 m², for four people.
  </p>

  <p>
    No suburban block has that ground, and no amount of design will conjure it.
    So the course does not ask you to close the loop. It asks you to measure{" "}
    <strong>how much of it you close, honestly counted</strong> — and to be
    equally precise about what still has to come through the front gate. Partial
    closure, stated accurately, is the deliverable.
  </p>

  <Figure
    caption="The loop the course teaches, and the two places it leaks. Dashed arrows are the imports and exports no design on these five sites removes."
    basis="model estimate"
  >
    <LoopDiagram />
  </Figure>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    You take one of five preset sites — a household of {household.occupants} on a
    real block, with real Bureau of Meteorology climate behind it — and design
    its food, water and waste systems over twelve weeks. Each week sizes one
    subsystem with a named method you can reproduce by hand, and hands you back a
    piece of the whole. By week 12 you hold a site master plan with mass balances
    that close, a bill of materials priced from a published schedule, and a list
    of the loads you were not certain about.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two numbers bound every decision: <strong>${constraints.budget_aud.toLocaleString("en-AU")}</strong> of
    new spend including a required {(constraints.contingency * 100).toFixed(0)} %
    contingency, and <strong>{constraints.energy_cap_kwh_day} kWh a day</strong> of
    process energy in the peak month, with uncertain loads counted at their upper
    estimate. Both are hard. The excellent reference design at the tightest site
    clears the budget by {`$${worst.reference.excellent.cost_headroom.toLocaleString("en-AU")}`},
    which tells you how much room there is to be wrong.
  </p>

  <h2>What you will not do</h2>
  <p>
    You will not build, operate, plumb, eat from or drink from anything. This is
    a design course, assessed on drawings, calculations and justified
    assumptions. Where the course sets a rule stricter than the law in your
    jurisdiction — and it often does — it says so, and says why.
  </p>
  <p>
    Energy and shelter are out of scope. Biogas is recorded and not credited;
    only the digestate's nutrients count. Those belong to <strong>{sequel.code} — {sequel.title}</strong>,
    a {sequel.status} that adds {sequel.adds}.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Fourth-year engineering and environmental-science students who want a design
    problem with a real constraint set rather than an open brief. You should
    arrive with:
  </p>
  <ul>
    {prerequisites.map((p) => <li>{p}</li>)}
  </ul>
  <p>
    If you have never sized anything against a monthly water balance, you will be
    fine. If you are looking for a course that concludes households can be
    self-sufficient, you will be disappointed by week 2.
  </p>

  <h2>The semester</h2>
  <p>
    Twelve teaching weeks from {formatCourseDate(courseMeta.startDate)} to{" "}
    {formatCourseDate(teachingEnds)}, with a two-week mid-semester break from{" "}
    {formatCourseDate(midSemesterBreak.from)}. The capstone falls in the
    assessment period afterwards, which is why the course record runs to{" "}
    {formatCourseDate(courseMeta.endDate)}.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title="The five sites" href="/sites/">
      <p>Canberra, Alice Springs, Brisbane, Adelaide and Darwin — parameters, inventory, and the strengths and constraints the model cannot score.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Method" href="/method/">
      <p>The indicators, the reference bands and what they are and are not: performance evidence inside a rubric, never an automatic grade.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Lectures" href="/lectures/">
      <p>Twelve weeks, each with one engineering question, one quantitative method and one worked example.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>What you bring to each studio, what happens in the room, and what leaves it changed.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>Five conceptual quizzes, the Food Loop Design and the capstone master plan.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Costing and energy" href="/method/costing/">
      <p>The price schedule every design is costed from, and exactly which loads count against the daily allowance.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .claim {
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
    padding-left: 1.1rem;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--at-primary);
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
  }
</style>
